===Etymology===
From {{inh|es|osp|nomne}}, {{m|osp|nomen}}, from a {{inh|es|VL.|*nōmine}}, from {{inh|es|la|nōmen}},<ref>https://www.scribd.com/document/158436269/Diccionario-Critico-Etimologico-castellano-MI-RI-Corominas-Joan-pdf</ref>, from {{inh|es|ine-pro|*h₁nómn̥}}. Compare {{cog|en|noun}}.

===Noun===
{{es-noun|m}}

# [[name]]
#: {{hyponyms|es|apellido}}
#: ''¿Cuál es tu '''nombre'''?'' — “What is your name?”
#: ''Mi '''nombre''' es ‘Carlos’.'' — “My name is ‘Carlos’.”
# {{lb|es|grammar}} [[noun]]

====Usage notes====
In Spanish, it is more common to use {{m|es|llamar|llamarse|to be called}} to indicate someone’s name:
: ''¿Cómo te llamas?'' — “What is your name?” (Literally, “What do you call yourself?”)
: ''Me llamo Carlos.'' — “My name is Carlos.” (Literally, “I call myself Carlos.”)

